msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:17
msgid ""
"// Private module, preventing users from accessing the `Sealed` trait.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:20
msgid ""
"// The trait itself must be public, or we can't use it as\n"
"    // part of our public API.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:24
msgid ""
"// Implement `Sealed` for types that should implement our\n"
"    // public public.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:29
msgid "// Use `Sealed` as the supertrait for our public trait.\n"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:32
msgid "/* methods */"
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:41
msgid ""
"Motivation: We want trait-driven code in a crate, but we don't want projects "
"that depend on this crate to be able to implement a trait."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:46
msgid ""
"The mechanism we use to do this is restricting access to a supertrait, "
"preventing downstream users from being able to implement that trait for "
"their types."
msgstr ""

#: src/idiomatic/polymorphism/generics/sealed-traits.md:50
msgid ""
"This is similar to enums, which inherently wrap a limited set of types, but "
"applies to traits, which by default are extensible."
msgstr ""
